This does not affect the licence of the app's source code. ODbL covers the
database, not the program that reads it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
USDA FoodData Central — js/food-library.js (BUNDLED)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
https://fdc.nal.usda.gov
Dataset: SR Legacy (2018-04)
Status: public domain — a work of the United States federal government,
        17 U.S.C. § 105. No licence conditions apply to its reuse.

194 generic foods with per-100 g values, generated by tools/build_foods.py from
the published bulk CSV. Each row records the exact source description in its
`usda` field so any number can be traced back.

This is bundled rather than queried because it can be: unlike Open Food Facts
above, redistributing it creates no derived-database obligation. It is also the
only way to offer search at all — Open Food Facts' free-text endpoints cannot be
called from a browser (no access-control-allow-origin), and its api/v2/search
silently ignores the search term rather than failing.

Attribution is not legally required for public-domain works. It is shown in the
app anyway, under Settings -> Credits and in the food library sheet, because a
number on screen should be able to say where it came from.
